<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rana Sanga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1482">1482</a>–<a href="/years/1528">1528</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sangram Singh I · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia-dynasty">Sisodia dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rana Sanga was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodia</a> ruler of Mewar from 1508 to 1528 and the leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> king of his time. He defeated the sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and built a wide coalition of Rajput clans. In 1527 he led this coalition against the Mughal founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-khanwa">Battle of Khanwa</a>, where he was defeated; he died the next year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rana-sanga.jpg" alt="Rana Sanga" width="320" height="511" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aristocratic 536 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photo_of_Rana_Sanga_from_Udaipur_Museum_(From_my_Camera).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sangram Singh was born to King Raimal and Queen Ratan Kunwar, a Jhala princess from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halvad">Halvad</a>. Contemporary texts give no year of birth, only the planetary positions at the time, from which the historian Gaurishankar Hirachand Ojha calculated 1482. Sanga was Raimal's third son, and he came to the throne of Mewar after a struggle with his brothers Prithviraj and Jagmal in which he lost an eye. He ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittor-fort">Chittorgarh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Sanga reunited the warring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> clans through diplomacy and marriage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> marched against him in 1517 and was defeated at Khatoli, where Sanga lost an arm and was lamed for life, and again at Dholpur in 1518–1519. Supporting Medini Rai, a rebel minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> sultan, Sanga beat the combined forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and Malwa at Gagron in 1519 and took eastern and northern Malwa and Chanderi, ordering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> tax on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> there removed.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> he restored Rai Mal to the throne of Idar, and in 1520 he invaded with 40,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a>, plundered the royal treasuries, destroyed several mosques and annexed Idar. At its height in 1521 his realm reached from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandu">Mandu</a> in the south to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> in the west.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-panipat">First Battle of Panipat</a> in 1526, Sanga united several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> clans and advanced towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>. He beat Babur's forces at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayana">Bayana</a> in February 1527, but on 16 March, at Khanwa, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> prevailed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlocks</a>. Struck by an arrow, Sanga was carried unconscious from the field, and Babur raised a tower of enemy skulls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sanga vowed not to return to Chittor until he had defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, but while preparing a new war he was poisoned by nobles who wanted no more fighting. He died at Kalpi in January or May 1528 and was succeeded by his son Ratan Singh II. Babur admired his reign, and the Mughal historian Abd al-Qadir Badayuni ranked him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithviraj-chauhan">Prithviraj Chauhan</a> as the bravest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a>. Legend credits him with a hundred battles and only one defeat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rana Sanga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1519 <b>Rana Sanga</b> of Chittor defeated a joint army of Malwa and Gujarat, later invaded Gujarat and plundered its treasuries; after his death the sultans recovered and sacked Chittor fort in 1535.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gujarat-sultanate">Gujarat Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1527 victory at the Battle of Khanwa over the Rajput confederacy led by <b>Rana Sanga</b> of Mewar secured his position and gave a durable imperial structure its basis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Rana Sanga</b> (r. c. 1508–1528) defeated Ibrahim Lodi at Khatoli in 1517 and captured Mahmud II of Malwa in 1519, and in 1527 he led a coalition of Rajput and Afghan forces against Babur, only to be defeated at the Battle of Khanwa by the Mughal combination of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chittorgarh, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Kalpi, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.89,74.62&amp;year=1511" aria-label="Chittorgarh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6530 1248 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rana Sanga"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6980 1518h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6980 1518h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.89,74.62&amp;year=1511">What was here in 1511? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
